learn:[
{icon:"🗺️",title:"India's States and Capitals",text:"India has 28 states, each with its own capital city serving as the centre of administration.",example:"Chennai is the capital of Tamil Nadu; Bhopal is the capital of Madhya Pradesh.",dyk:"Some states, like Andhra Pradesh, have had their capitals changed over time.",real:"Knowing your neighbouring states and their capitals builds practical geographic awareness."},
{icon:"🏛️",title:"Union Territories of India",text:"India has 8 union territories, administered directly by the central government.",example:"Delhi and Chandigarh are well-known union territories.",dyk:"Delhi has a partially elected government, unlike fully centrally-administered territories.",real:"Visiting Delhi, the National Capital Territory, shows how a union territory functions."},
{icon:"🌏",title:"Regional Groupings of States",text:"India's states are often grouped into regions like North, South, East, West and Northeast.",example:"Assam, Manipur and Nagaland are part of the Northeast region.",dyk:"The Northeast region is known for its unique cultures and stunning natural landscapes.",real:"Planning a trip often involves thinking about which regional group of states to visit."},
{icon:"🏔️",title:"States by Geographic Features",text:"States can be classified by dominant geographic features like coastal, hilly, or plains regions.",example:"Kerala is a coastal state; Himachal Pradesh is a hilly state.",dyk:"India has states with vastly different climates, from cold Himalayas to hot desert.",real:"Understanding a state's geography helps predict its climate and agriculture."},
{icon:"📊",title:"Interesting State Facts",text:"Each Indian state has unique facts about its size, population, culture and history.",example:"Rajasthan is the largest state by area; Goa is the smallest by area.",dyk:"Uttar Pradesh has the largest population among all Indian states.",real:"Learning fun facts about different states makes geography more engaging."}
],
